{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "29",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 70s with no history of chronic or allergic rhinosinusitis presented to the emergency department with a headache for 2 weeks and vision loss in the right eye for 2 days. Three weeks prior, he had rhinorrhea and was diagnosed with COVID-19. He recovered from COVID-19 but developed a worsening right-sided headache 1 week later and right vision loss 2 days prior to presentation.\n\nRight eye visual acuity was limited to hand-motion detection, with severe pain on ocular motion.\n\n{'Imaging Studies': {'Maxillofacial CT': {'Findings': 'Opacification of all sinuses on the right with diffuse inflammation. Bony erosion of the medial orbital apex bone, with extension of inflammation into the apex', 'Images': {'Title': 'Contrast-Enhanced Computed Tomographic Scans', 'Description': 'Complete opacification of the (A) right frontal sinus, (B) right ethmoid sinus with erosion of the medial orbital apex bone, extension of inflammation into the apex, and an air-fluid level in the sphenoid sinus, and (C) right maxillary sinus'}}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Serum IgG4': '277 mg/dL (normal, <123 mg/dL)', 'Sinus Cultures': 'Positive for Streptococcus constellatus'}, 'Pathology': {'Findings': 'Dense infiltrate of IgG-containing plasma cells, with most being IgG4 positive', 'Images': {'Title': 'Histopathologic Images', 'Description': 'A, Dense band-like infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells present beneath the epithelium. B, IgG stain demonstrates 50 to 100 plasma cells in many high-power fields. C, Most of the IgG-containing plasma cells were IgG4 positive'}}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. IgG4-related rhinosinusitis with concurrent Streptococcus constellatus bacterial rhinosinusitis\nB. IgG4-related disease\nC. IgG4-Related Disease (IgG4-RD) with Orbital/Sinus Involvement\nD. Invasive bacterial rhinosinusitis with orbital apex syndrome\n",
